  "summary": "Storage class specifiers in C provide the compiler with crucial information regarding variable storage location, initial values, and variable lifetimes. Their general declaration syntax consists of the storage specifier, followed by the data type and variable name. The auto specifier declares automatic variables, which reside in CPU memory and possess local scope. These variables are not initialized automatically and must be initialized explicitly upon declaration. Attempting to declare an auto variable at file scope outside a function causes a compilation error, specifically indicating that a filescope declaration specifies auto, because auto variables cannot be declared globally. In contrast, static variables are automatically initialized to zero and their storage space is also CPU memory. Unlike automatic variables, static variables retain their values across distinct function calls and are not destroyed when program execution control leaves the enclosing block. Demonstrations illustrate the declaration, compilation, and error handling of auto variables alongside the properties of static variables using Ubuntu Linux and the GCC compiler.",
